<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1163">1163</a>–<a href="/years/1184">1184</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Yaqub Yusuf I was the second <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>, son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a>. He consolidated Almohad rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> and was a patron of learning, supporting philosophers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl">Ibn Tufayl</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Ibn Rushd</a> (Averroes). He also sponsored building works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, and he died in 1184 during a campaign in Portugal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-yaqub-yusuf-i.jpg" alt="Abu Yaqub Yusuf I" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MoneyMuseum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abu_Yaqub_Yusef_Coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Yaqub Yusuf was born in 1135, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin">Abd al-Mu'min</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> caliph. His mother was Safiyya bint Abi Imran, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masmuda">Masmuda</a> woman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinmel">Tinmel</a> and the daughter of Abu Imran Musa ibn Sulayman al-Kafif, a companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tumart">Ibn Tumart</a>. He supported Almohad doctrine and, like his predecessors, favoured the literalist Zahiri school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-jurisprudence">Islamic jurisprudence</a>. He was a religious scholar in his own right and was said to have memorised the Sahih Bukhari and Sahih Muslim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Yusuf reigned from 1163 until 1184 from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>. He continued his father's conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and Catalonia, and in 1171 he established himself in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>. He ordered numerous buildings there, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcazar-of-seville">Alcázar of Seville</a>, the Buhaira Gardens and the fortress of Alcalá de Guadaíra, and he was responsible for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giralda">Giralda</a>, part of a new grand mosque. Respected men of letters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/averroes">Ibn Rushd</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-tufayl">Ibn Tufayl</a> were entertained at his court. He favoured the Córdoban polymath ibn Maḍāʾ as his chief judge, and the two oversaw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> reforms that banned religious material written by non-Zahiris. His son al-Mansur later went further and burned non-Zahiri books.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Yusuf was wounded at the Siege of Santarém in 1184 and died on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a>, on 14 October 1184. His body was taken from Seville to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinmel">Tinmel</a> and buried there. His buildings in Seville remained, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giralda">Giralda</a> in particular is attributed to him. The arrival of the zealous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> is also described as having ended the golden age of Jewish culture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Yaqub Yusuf” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The absorption of its remaining independent Muslim principalities, the last being the kingdom of Ibn Mardanish in Murcia and Valencia, was completed under his son <b>Abu Yaqub Yusuf I</b> in 1172.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinmel">Tinmel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.99,-8.23&amp;year=1173" aria-label="Tinmel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4337 1034 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abu Yaqub Yusuf I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4787 1304h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4787 1304h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.99,-8.23&amp;year=1173">What was here in 1173? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
